+# R53 — bounded receiver-store I/O retry
+
+## Finding
+
+R52 publishes a one-shot readiness result. `ReceiverDetectionRuntime.initialize()` used `runCatching` around the first `AtomicFile.openRead()` / initial `persist()`, then permanently completed the readiness gate with `false` on any exception. A transient storage I/O failure therefore disabled receiver-scoped configuration for the remainder of the process even if the file system recovered moments later.
+
+## Change
+
+- Added `ReceiverStoreIoRetry`, which retries only `IOException`, at most three total attempts, with 100 ms then 250 ms backoff.
+- The first-time empty-store write is converted to `IOException` if `persist()` returns false, so transient AtomicFile failures use the same bounded retry path.
+- JSON/schema/secret/field validation failures are not `IOException` and fail immediately; interrupted I/O is not retried. If the backoff sleep is interrupted, the thread interrupt flag is restored and an `InterruptedIOException` is thrown.
+- The redacted `ReceiverDetectionRuntime.reportSummary()` includes `storeRetries`; it never exposes the receiver address, HMAC binding, or secret.
+- The one-shot readiness contract is unchanged: after all retries fail, this process remains not-ready. No raw address or fallback identity is created.
+
+## Tests
+
+- transient read error followed by success;
+- two transient errors followed by success and exact bounded backoff;
+- malformed store fail-fast without retry;
+- retry exhaustion after three attempts;
+- `InterruptedIOException` fail-fast;
+- interruption during backoff preserves the interrupt flag.
+
+## Limits
+
+This only improves local configuration-store startup resilience. It does not change iAP2 wire behavior, MFi verification, 0x4300 body shape, retransmission, SYN/DETECT handling, 0x4301 acceptance, Wi-Fi handoff or AirPlay. Full Android Gradle build and E2LB real-vehicle validation remain required.
